    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7026(f)(1) and \u00a7 7026(f)(3)(A), a business must cease selling or sharing data as soon as feasibly possible, with 15 business days being the maximum upper limit. For real-time bidding where data transfers can be restricted instantaneously, the business must honor the opt-out request immediately upon receipt.",
    "generated_answer": "No, an online publisher that utilizes programmatic advertising technology for real-time bidding cannot wait the full 15 business days if it is technically feasible to stop the sale/sharing sooner.\n\nUnder **\u00a7 7026(f)(1)**, a business must comply with a request to opt-out of sale/sharing \"as soon as feasibly possible, but no later than 15 business days.\" \n\nThe regulations provide a specific illustrative example regarding this requirement in **\u00a7 7026(f)(3)(A)**:\n\n*   **Real-Time Bidding Context**: A business using programmatic advertising that instantaneously sells and shares personal information through real-time bidding is capable of restricting the transfer of data instantaneously.\n*   **Requirement**: Because the business can stop the sale/sharing immediately, it is not permitted to take 15 business days to comply. It must comply immediately upon receiving the request.\n\nIn summary, the 15-business-day window is a maximum limit; if the business\u2019s technology allows for immediate cessation of the sale or sharing of data, the business is legally required to stop immediately (**\u00a7 7026(f)(3)(A)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.893,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, accurately cites the relevant sections of the CCPA regulations (\u00a7 7026), and correctly interprets the 'feasibility' standard as it applies to real-time bidding. It perfectly aligns with the ground truth by emphasizing that the 15-day window is a maximum limit and that immediate compliance is required when technically feasible.",
