    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7053(b), whether a business conducts due diligence factors into whether it had reason to believe the third party was violating the CCPA. A business that never enforces the terms of its contract might not be able to rely on the defense that it lacked reason to believe the third party intended to use the personal information in violation of CCPA regulations.",
    "generated_answer": "A business's failure to enforce its contract terms significantly impacts its ability to establish a defense regarding the misuse of personal information by a third party.\n\nUnder **\u00a7 7053(b)**, the following rules apply:\n\n*   **Impact on Due Diligence**: Whether a business conducts due diligence regarding its third-party partners is a factor in determining whether the business had \"reason to believe\" that a third party was using personal information in violation of the CCPA and its regulations.\n*   **Defense Limitation**: If a business never enforces the terms of its contract, it may be unable to rely on the defense that it lacked \"reason to believe\" that the third party intended to use the personal information in violation of the CCPA at the time the business disclosed that information. \n\nIn short, a history of non-enforcement undermines a business's claim that it acted in good faith or without knowledge of the third party's non-compliant practices, as defined under **\u00a7 7053(b)**.",
