  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7004_003",
    "query": "Can a navigation app require a user to consent to selling their location data to third-party data brokers in order to use the app's basic GPS mapping features?",
    "target_section": "7004",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": false,
    "hit_in_top_1": false,
    "precision_at_k": 0.0,
    "key_term_recall": 0.2,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7004(a)(4)(B), bundling choices so that a consumer must consent to incompatible secondary uses (e.g., selling geolocation to data brokers) together with necessary operational uses (e.g., providing location-based navigation) is a prohibited choice architecture. The business must provide a separate, unbundled option for consent to incompatible secondary uses.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, the documentation does not contain specific rules regarding the permissibility of conditioning the use of a navigation app's basic GPS features on the consent to sell location data to third-party data brokers.\n\nHowever, the provided text does establish rules regarding **financial incentive programs** and consumer privacy choices that may be relevant to how businesses interact with users regarding the sale/sharing of personal information:\n\n*   **Financial Incentive Programs**: Under **\u00a7 7025(b)(4)**, if a business maintains a financial incentive program that requires a consumer to consent to the sale or sharing of personal information, the business must allow the consumer to opt out of such sale/sharing. If the business processes an opt-out preference signal, it may notify the consumer that doing so would withdraw them from the financial incentive program and ask the consumer to affirm their intent to withdraw.\n*   **Opt-out Preference Signals**: Under **\u00a7 7025(b)(1)**, a business must treat an opt-out preference signal as a valid request to opt out of the sale/sharing of personal information. If a business offers a service, it generally cannot ignore these signals unless there is a specific conflict with a financial incentive program as defined in **\u00a7 7025(b)(4)**.\n*   **Third-Party Contracts**: If a business does sell or share personal information with a third party, it must enter into a contract that identifies the limited and specified purpose for the data, requires the third party to comply with the CCPA, and grants the business the right to take steps to ensure the third party acts consistently with the business\u2019s obligations (**\u00a7 7053(a)**).\n\n**Limitations of this response:**\nThe provided text does not define \"basic features\" or specify the legality of \"pay-for-privacy\" or \"consent-or-pay\" models for utility-based applications (like GPS navigation). Furthermore, it does not explicitly address whether location data specifically must be provided access-free or if it can be tied to a broader consent requirement beyond the specific context of financial incentive programs.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.7732,
    "llm_judge_score": 1.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer failed to identify the relevant regulation (\u00a7 7004(a)(4)(B)) provided in the ground truth, which explicitly addresses the prohibition of bundling incompatible secondary uses with necessary operational uses. Instead, the candidate incorrectly claimed the documentation did not contain specific rules regarding this scenario and pivoted to irrelevant sections regarding financial incentives and opt-out signals, leading to a failure to answer the core legal question.",
    "model_refusal": false,
    "execution_time_sec": 7.476,
    "retrieved_sources": [
      {
        "parent_id": "v2026_sec7053_p1",
        "section": "7053",
        "section_title": "Contract Requirements for Third Parties.",
        "rerank_score": 0.0189,
        "snippet": "\u00a7 7053. Contract Requirements for Third Parties.\n\u00a7 7053. Contract Requirements for Third Parties.\n\n(a)\nA business that sells or shares a consumer\u2019s personal information with a thir..."
      },
      {
        "parent_id": "v2026_sec7012_p2",
        "section": "7012",
        "section_title": "Notice at Collection of Personal Information.",
        "rerank_score": 0.0049,
        "snippet": "\u00a7 7012. Notice at Collection of Personal Information.\n(g) Third Parties that Control the Collection of Personal Information. This\nsubsection shall not affect the first party\u2019s obli..."
      },
      {
        "parent_id": "v2026_sec7025_p2",
