    "hit_in_top_1": false,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7221(c)(4), a cookie banner or cookie control tool is explicitly prohibited from serving as an acceptable ADMT opt-out method because cookie tools pertain to data collection, not the automated processing of decisionmaking technology. ADMT opt-out methods must be specific to the right to opt-out of ADMT.",
    "generated_answer": "No, a business cannot rely solely on a standard website cookie banner or cookie management tool to handle requests to opt-out of Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT).\n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7221(c)(4)**, a notification or tool regarding cookies (such as a cookie banner or cookie controls) is not, by itself, an acceptable method for submitting requests to opt-out of the business\u2019s use of ADMT. This is because cookie-related tools concern the collection of personal information, whereas an acceptable method for ADMT opt-out requests must be specific to the right to opt-out of the business\u2019s use of ADMT.\n\nTo comply with the regulations regarding ADMT opt-out requests, a business must adhere to the following:\n\n*   **Designated Methods**: A business must provide two or more designated methods for submitting requests to opt-out, and at least one must reflect the manner in which the business primarily interacts with the consumer (**\u00a7 7221(c)**).\n*   **Online Requirements**: For businesses interacting with consumers online, at least one method must be an interactive form accessible via an opt-out link provided in the Pre-use Notice. The link title must clearly state what the consumer is opting out of (e.g., \u201cOpt-out of Automated Decisionmaking Technology\u201d) (**\u00a7 7221(c)(1)**).\n*   **Ease of Use**: The methods must be easy for consumers to execute, require minimal steps, and comply with \u00a7 7004 (**\u00a7 7221(d)**).\n*   **Verification**: A business must **not** require a verifiable consumer request for an ADMT opt-out request (**\u00a7 7221(f)** and **\u00a7 7060(b)**). While a business may ask for information necessary to identify the consumer, it must not require the consumer to create an account or provide unnecessary information (**\u00a7 7221(e)** and **\u00a7 7221(f)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9183,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, fully aligned with the ground truth, and provides additional relevant regulatory context regarding the requirements for ADMT opt-out methods that enhances the completeness of the response without introducing hallucinations.",
