    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7302(c)(1), probable cause proceedings are closed to the public by default and conducted in whole or in part by telephone or videoconference. An alleged violator may make the proceeding public or in-person ONLY by filing a written request at least 10 business days prior to the proceeding.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, here are the requirements regarding the accessibility of probable cause proceedings:\n\n### Public Accessibility\nProbable cause proceedings are **not** open to the public by default. According to **\u00a7 7302(c)(1)**, the proceeding must be closed to the public and conducted via telephone or videoconference.\n\n### Requesting an In-Person or Public Proceeding\nAn alleged violator may request an in-person or public proceeding by adhering to the following requirement:\n\n*   **Written Request**: The alleged violator must file a written request for an in-person or public proceeding with the Agency (**\u00a7 7302(c)(1)**).\n*   **Deadline**: This request must be filed at least **10 business days** before the date of the proceeding (**\u00a7 7302(c)(1)**).\n\nPlease note that per **\u00a7 7302(c)(2)**, participation in these proceedings is strictly limited to the alleged violator(s), their legal counsel, and the Enforcement Division. If an alleged violator fails to attend the scheduled proceeding, they waive their right to further probable cause proceedings, and the Agency will determine probable cause based on the existing notice and arguments provided by the Enforcement Division (**\u00a7 7302(c)(3)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9127,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, fully aligned with the ground truth, and provides additional relevant context from the regulation without introducing hallucinations. It correctly identifies the default closed nature of the proceedings and the specific procedural requirements for requesting a public or in-person hearing.",
