    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7001(cc), 'performance at work' explicitly excludes a consumer's location when off-duty or on breaks, as well as their use of a personal account (e.g., email, text messages, or social media). Personal account monitoring is only permitted if conducted solely to prevent/limit account use on company systems or to prevent confidential information disclosures.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, an employer **cannot** classify a consumer\u2019s personal social media activity or off-duty location tracking as \"performance at work.\"\n\nUnder **\u00a7 7001(cc)**, \"performance at work\" is defined as the performance of job duties for which the consumer has been hired or has applied to be hired. The section explicitly states that the following are **not** considered \"performance at work\":\n\n*   **Off-duty location:** A consumer\u2019s location when off-duty or on breaks.\n*   **Personal accounts:** A consumer\u2019s use of a personal account, such as social media (or email/text messages), unless the monitoring is done \"solely to prevent or limit the use of these accounts on the business\u2019s information system or to prevent the disclosure of confidential information.\"\n\n**Regulatory Scope Note:**\nThe provided text covers the definition of \"performance at work\" and the scope of cybersecurity audits. It does not contain broader provisions regarding general employment law or privacy rights outside of the CCPA/CPRA context provided. Therefore, this answer is limited to how these specific activities are defined under \u00a7 7001(cc).",
