    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7026(k) and \u00a7 7027(l), a business must wait at least 12 months from the date of the consumer's request before asking the consumer to consent to the sale/sharing or expanded use of sensitive personal information.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, a business must wait at least 12 months from the date of the consumer's request before asking the consumer to opt back in or consent to the use/disclosure of their information.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n*   **Opt-out of Sale or Sharing**: Pursuant to **\u00a7 7026(k)**, a business shall wait at least 12 months from the date of the consumer\u2019s request before asking a consumer who has opted out of the sale or sharing of their personal information to consent to such sale or sharing.\n*   **Limiting Use of Sensitive Personal Information**: Pursuant to **\u00a7 7027(l)**, a business shall wait at least 12 months from the date the consumer\u2019s request to limit is received before asking a consumer who has exercised their right to limit to consent to the use or disclosure of their sensitive personal information (for purposes other than those set forth in **\u00a7 7027(m)**).\n\n**Note on the Opt-in Process:**\nWhen a business does eventually seek consent after this period, it must follow the requirements set forth in **\u00a7 7028**:\n*   The business must use a \"two-step opt-in process\" where the consumer first clearly requests to opt-in and subsequently confirms that choice (**\u00a7 7028(a)**).\n*   The business must comply with the requirements in **\u00a7 7004** when obtaining this consent (**\u00a7 7028(b)** and **(c)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8886,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
