  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7221_002",
    "query": "Under what narrow conditions can a business deny an ADMT opt-out request for employee hiring or work allocation decisions without providing a human appeal option?",
    "target_section": "7221",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.25,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7221(b)(2)\u2013(3), opt-out exceptions apply to hiring/admission or work allocation/compensation decisions ONLY IF: (1) the ADMT is used solely to assess the consumer's ability to perform at work/school or solely for work allocation/compensation; AND (2) the ADMT demonstrably works for the business's purpose and does not unlawfully discriminate based on protected characteristics.",
    "generated_answer": "Under the provided regulations, a business is not required to provide consumers with the ability to opt-out of the use of Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT) for significant hiring or work allocation decisions, nor are they required to provide a human appeal process, if they meet the specific criteria outlined in **\u00a7 7221(b)**.\n\nThe narrow conditions are as follows:\n\n### 1. Admission, Acceptance, or Hiring Decisions\nFor decisions regarding admission, acceptance, or hiring (as set forth in **\u00a7 7001(ddd)(3)(A)** and **(ddd)(4)(A)**), a business is exempt if:\n*   The business uses the ADMT **solely** to assess the consumer\u2019s ability to perform at work or in an educational program for the purpose of determining whether to admit, accept, or hire them (**\u00a7 7221(b)(2)(A)**).\n*   The ADMT works for the business\u2019s intended purpose and does not unlawfully discriminate based upon protected characteristics (**\u00a7 7221(b)(2)(B)**).\n\n### 2. Allocation/Assignment of Work and Compensation\nFor decisions regarding the allocation or assignment of work and compensation (as set forth in **\u00a7 7001(ddd)(4)(B)**), a business is exempt if:\n*   The business uses the ADMT **solely** for the allocation or assignment of work or compensation (**\u00a7 7221(b)(3)(A)**).\n*   The ADMT works for the business\u2019s intended purpose and does not unlawfully discriminate based upon protected characteristics (**\u00a7 7221(b)(3)(B)**).\n\n***\n\n**Important Compliance Note:** \nIf a business relies on these specific exceptions to deny an opt-out request, it must still fulfill its disclosure obligations. Under **\u00a7 7220(c)(2)(B)**, the business\u2019s **Pre-use Notice** must explicitly identify the specific exception from **\u00a7 7221(b)** that it is relying upon.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8752,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, highly structured, and provides a more comprehensive breakdown of the regulatory requirements than the ground truth while remaining entirely consistent with it. It correctly identifies the specific subsections of the CCPA regulations and includes a helpful compliance note regarding disclosure obligations.",
