    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7153(a)\u2013(b), a business that makes ADMT (trained using personal information) available to another recipient business to make a significant decision concerning a consumer pursuant to \u00a7 7150(b)(3) MUST provide to the recipient business all facts available to it that are necessary for the recipient business to conduct its own risk assessment.",
    "generated_answer": "Under California privacy regulations, a business that makes Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT) available to a \"recipient-business\" for the purpose of making a significant decision (as defined in \u00a7 7150, subsection (b)(3)) must facilitate the recipient-business's compliance requirements.\n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7153(a)**, the vendor must provide to the recipient-business:\n* **All available facts** that are necessary for the recipient-business to conduct its own risk assessment.\n\n**Applicability Note:**\n* These requirements apply specifically to ADMT that has been trained using personal information (**\u00a7 7153(b)**).\n\n**Context for the Recipient-Business:**\nWhen performing the risk assessment that the vendor's data helps facilitate, the recipient-business is required by **\u00a7 7152(a)(3)(G)** to identify the following elements regarding the ADMT:\n* The **logic** of the ADMT, including any assumptions or limitations of that logic; and\n* The **output** of the ADMT and how the business will use that output to make a significant decision.\n\nThe provided regulatory context does not contain an exhaustive list of specific data points the vendor must share, other than the general mandate to provide all facts \"necessary for the recipient-business to conduct its own risk assessment\" as specified in **\u00a7 7153(a)**.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8867,
