    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Yes. Under \u00a7 7271(b)(1), when an insurance company collects personal information from website visitors who have not applied for an insurance product or service and uses it to tailor cross-context targeted ads, that data is not related to an insurance transaction under Insurance Code \u00a7 791.02. Therefore, the company must fully comply with CCPA, including honoring opt-out preference signals and providing sale/sharing opt-out rights.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, here are the requirements regarding opt-out preference signals and the right to opt-out of sale/sharing:\n\n*   **Applicability**: The regulations apply to any business that \"sells or shares personal information\" (\u00a7 7025(b), \u00a7 7026(a)). If an insurance company sells or shares the personal information of website visitors\u2014regardless of whether they have applied for a product\u2014it must comply with these requirements.\n*   **Requirement to Honor Signals**: A business that sells or shares personal information must treat a valid opt-out preference signal as a request to opt-out of sale/sharing for that browser, device, and any associated consumer profile (\u00a7 7025(b)(1)).\n*   **Definition of Valid Signal**: For a signal to be valid, it must be in a format commonly used and recognized by businesses (e.g., an HTTP header field or JavaScript object), and the technology sending the signal must clearly disclose that it is meant to function as an opt-out of the sale and sharing of personal information (\u00a7 7025(b)).\n*   **Required Methods for Opt-Out**: A business that collects personal information online must, at a minimum, allow consumers to submit requests to opt-out of sale/sharing through:\n    *   An opt-out preference signal; and\n    *   At least one additional method, such as an interactive form accessible via a \u201cDo Not Sell or Share My Personal Information\u201d link or an Alternative Opt-out Link (\u00a7 7026(a)(1)).\n*   **Processing Requests**:\n    *   The business must not require the consumer to provide additional information beyond what is necessary to send the signal or process the request (\u00a7 7025(b)(2), \u00a7 7026(c)).\n    *   The business must cease selling or sharing the consumer\u2019s personal information as soon as feasibly possible, but no later than 15 business days from the date the request is received (\u00a7 7026(f)(1)).\n    *   The business must notify third parties to whom it has sold or shared the information (after the request was submitted but before compliance) to also stop selling or sharing that information (\u00a7 7026(f)(2)).\n\n**Scope Limitation:**\nThe provided context explains the obligations for businesses that sell or share personal information under the CCPA/CPRA. It does not provide specific exemptions for insurance companies based on their industry status; therefore, the obligations depend entirely on whether the business meets the definition of a business that sells or shares personal information.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8032,
    "llm_judge_score": 4.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally accurate regarding the general CCPA requirements for opt-out signals and sale/sharing. However, it misses the specific nuance provided in the ground truth regarding the intersection of the CCPA and the California Insurance Code (\u00a7 791.02). While the candidate correctly identifies that there is no industry-wide exemption, it fails to explicitly address the specific regulatory reasoning (the distinction between data related to an insurance transaction vs. general website visitor data) that clarifies why the CCPA applies in this specific insurance context.",
    "model_refusal": false,
