    "eval_id": "eval_7025_004",
    "query": "What three specific user interface restrictions must a business satisfy to process an opt-out preference signal in a 'frictionless manner' under section 7025(f)?",
    "target_section": "7025",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.3333,
    "key_term_recall": 0.4,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7025(f)(1)\u2013(3), processing an opt-out preference signal in a frictionless manner requires that the business shall NOT: (1) Charge a fee or require valuable consideration; (2) Change or degrade the consumer's experience or product functionality compared to non-signal users; and (3) Display any notification, pop-up, text, graphic, animation, sound, video, or interstitial content in response to the signal (other than displaying the signal status or a link to a privacy settings menu).",
    "generated_answer": "To process an opt-out preference signal in a \"frictionless manner\" under **\u00a7 7025(f)**, a business must satisfy the following three specific user interface and operational restrictions:\n\n*   **No Fees or Consideration:** The business shall not charge a fee or require any valuable consideration from the consumer for using an opt-out preference signal (**\u00a7 7025(f)(1)**).\n*   **No Change to User Experience:** The business shall not change the consumer\u2019s experience with the product or service. The product or service must function the same for a consumer who uses an opt-out preference signal as it does for a consumer who does not use one (**\u00a7 7025(f)(2)**).\n*   **No Prohibited Interstitial Content:** The business shall not display a notification, pop-up, text, graphic, animation, sound, video, or any other interstitial content in response to the opt-out preference signal (**\u00a7 7025(f)(3)**).\n\n**Note on permitted UI elements:** While the above restrictions apply, **\u00a7 7025(f)(3)** clarifies that a business is not prohibited from displaying whether a consumer has opted out of the sale or sharing of their personal information. Additionally, a business may provide a link to a privacy settings page, menu, or similar interface that allows a consumer to consent to the business ignoring the signal, provided that the interface itself remains compliant with the requirements in **\u00a7 7025(f)(1)\u2013(3)**.",
    "semantic_similarity": null,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, complete, and accurately reflects the requirements of \u00a7 7025(f). It correctly identifies the three restrictions and includes the necessary nuance regarding permitted UI elements found in the regulation.",
