  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7012_003",
    "query": "If a retail store or website allows a third party (such as a Wi-Fi provider or ad network) to collect personal data on its premises or site, who must provide the Notice at Collection?",
    "target_section": "7012",
    "expected_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.25,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7012(g)(1)\u2013(3), BOTH the first party and the third party controlling data collection have an obligation to provide a Notice at Collection. They may provide a single joint notice, or the third party must provide its own conspicuous notice (e.g., on its homepage, the Wi-Fi connection landing interface, or dashboard signage in rental vehicles).",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, both the first-party business and the third party that controls the collection of personal information have obligations regarding the Notice at Collection.\n\n### General Obligation\nUnder **\u00a7 7012(g)(1)**, when more than one business controls the collection of a consumer's personal information (such as a first party allowing a third party to collect data on its website), **both the first party and the third party are required to provide a Notice at Collection.** \n\nBusinesses have the option to provide a single, joint Notice at Collection that includes the required information regarding their collective practices (**\u00a7 7012(g)(1)**).\n\n### Specific Operational Requirements\nThe regulations provide specific guidance on how this must be implemented based on the environment:\n\n*   **Online/Website Collection:**\n    *   **First Party:** May post a conspicuous link to its Notice at Collection on its homepage (**\u00a7 7012(g)(3)(A)**).\n    *   **Third Party:** Shall either provide its own Notice at Collection on its homepage or include its information practices within the first party\u2019s Notice at Collection (**\u00a7 7012(g)(3)(A)**).\n*   **Physical Premises (Retail/Wi-Fi):**\n    *   **Wi-Fi Services:** A business providing Wi-Fi services (the third party) must post its Notice at Collection on the first webpage or interface a consumer sees before connecting to the service. The first party (e.g., a coffee shop) may fulfill its obligation by posting conspicuous signage at the store entrance or point-of-sale directing consumers to where its own Notice at Collection is found online (**\u00a7 7012(g)(3)(B)**).\n    *   **General Physical Presence:** A third party controlling collection in a physical location must provide the Notice at Collection in a conspicuous manner at the physical location where it is collecting the information (**\u00a7 7012(g)(2)**).\n    *   **Vehicle Examples:** A business allowing a third party to collect data within a vehicle may provide its notice in writing or orally at the point of sale, while the third party may use signage (e.g., on a dashboard) to direct consumers to where its notice is available online (**\u00a7 7012(g)(3)(C)**).\n\n### Important Considerations\n*   **Opt-Out Obligations:** Providing a Notice at Collection does not absolve a first-party business of its CCPA obligations to comply with a consumer\u2019s request to opt-out of sale or sharing (**\u00a7 7012(g)**).\n*   **Contractual Requirements:** If the first-party business shares information with these third parties, it must ensure it has an agreement in place that meets the requirements of **\u00a7 7053**, which mandates that the third party uses the data only for specified, limited purposes and complies with CCPA regulations.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8867,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, highly comprehensive, and perfectly aligned with the ground truth. It correctly identifies the dual obligation of both the first and third parties under \u00a7 7012(g) and provides helpful, accurate context regarding the implementation of these requirements in both digital and physical environments.",
    "model_refusal": false,
    "execution_time_sec": 6.76,
