    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.1667,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Yes. Under \u00a7 7001(bbb)(4), personal information of consumers that the business has actual knowledge are less than 16 years of age is classified as sensitive personal information. Furthermore, a business that willfully disregards the consumer's age is legally deemed to have had actual knowledge of their age.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, here is the answer to your query:\n\n### Qualification as Sensitive Personal Information\nYes, the personal information of a 15-year-old consumer qualifies as \"sensitive personal information.\" \n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7001(bbb)(4)**, sensitive personal information includes:\n* \"Personal information of consumers that the business has actual knowledge are less than 16 years of age.\"\n\n### Legal Standard for Age Knowledge\nThe regulations apply a strict standard regarding a business's claim of ignorance regarding a consumer's age:\n\n* **Willful Disregard**: Under **\u00a7 7001(bbb)(4)**, if a business \"willfully disregards the consumer\u2019s age,\" it shall be deemed to have had actual knowledge of the consumer's age. \n\nThis means a business cannot avoid the classification of the information as sensitive simply by claiming it did not know the consumer was under 16 if it purposefully ignored the age of the consumer.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9043,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, accurately cites the relevant regulation (\u00a7 7001(bbb)(4)), and correctly explains both the classification of the information and the legal standard regarding willful disregard. It aligns perfectly with the ground truth.",
