    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7122(d), no finding of a cybersecurity audit may rely primarily on management assertions or attestations; findings must rely primarily on specific evidence (documents, sampling, technical testing, interviews). Under \u00a7 7122(g), both the business and auditor must retain all relevant audit documents for a minimum of five (5) years after completion.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, here are the requirements regarding audit reliance and document retention:\n\n### Reliance on Management Attestations\nNo, a cybersecurity audit report **may not** rely primarily on assertions or attestations by the business\u2019s management. \n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7122(d)**, cybersecurity audit findings must rely primarily upon specific evidence, which includes:\n*   Documents reviewed;\n*   Sampling and testing performed; and\n*   Interviews conducted by the auditor.\n\n### Document Retention\nThe business and the auditor are required to retain all documentation relevant to each cybersecurity audit for a minimum of **five (5) years** after the completion of the audit (**\u00a7 7122(g)**).",
