  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7004_002",
    "query": "If a business unintentionally designs a user interface that impairs consumer choice, can it still be classified as a dark pattern, and what is the legal effect on any consent obtained?",
    "target_section": "7004",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.5,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Yes. Under \u00a7 7004(c), a business's intent is not determinative; if the interface substantially subverts or impairs user autonomy, decisionmaking, or choice, and the business knows of and does not remedy it, it may still be a dark pattern. Under \u00a7 7004(b), any agreement obtained through a dark pattern does not constitute valid consumer consent (the business is in the position of never having obtained consent).",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory text, here is the answer to your query:\n\n### Classification of \"Unintentional\" Dark Patterns\nYes, a user interface can still be classified as a dark pattern even if the business did not intend for it to impair consumer choice. According to **\u00a7 7004(c)**:\n*   A user interface is considered a dark pattern if it has the effect of substantially subverting or impairing user autonomy, decisionmaking, or choice.\n*   A business's intent in designing the interface is not the determinative factor.\n*   If a business did not originally intend to impair user choice but later becomes aware of an interface that has that effect and fails to remedy it, that interface is classified as a dark pattern.\n\n### Legal Effect on Consent\nIf a business uses a dark pattern to obtain consumer consent, the legal consequences are as follows:\n*   **Invalid Consent**: Any agreement obtained through the use of dark patterns does not constitute valid consumer consent (**\u00a7 7004(b)**).\n*   **Resulting Status**: If a business uses dark patterns to obtain consent to sell a consumer\u2019s personal information, the business is legally positioned as if it had never obtained the consumer\u2019s consent at all (**\u00a7 7004(b)**).\n\n***\n\n*Note: The provided text does not define the broader legal penalties for non-compliance beyond the invalidation of consent, nor does it address specific remediation timelines beyond the requirement that a business must \"remedy\" a known interface that impairs choice.*",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8968,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, fully aligned with the ground truth, and accurately cites the relevant sections of the CCPA regulations. It correctly identifies that intent is not determinative and clearly explains the legal consequence regarding the invalidity of consent.",
    "model_refusal": false,
    "execution_time_sec": 8.309,
    "retrieved_sources": [
      {
