  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7025_008",
    "query": "When an unauthenticated guest visits a website using an opt-out preference signal, how far does the business's duty to process the opt-out extend?",
    "target_section": "7025",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": true,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 1.0,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7025(c)(1) and Example (A) in \u00a7 7025(c)(7), for an unauthenticated guest, the business must apply the opt-out preference signal to that specific browser or device and any pseudonymous profile tied to that browser identifier. The business is not required to apply the opt-out to any offline data or account profiles if the link between the browser and the consumer's account is unknown.",
    "generated_answer": "When an unauthenticated guest (or a consumer not otherwise known to the business) visits a website using an opt-out preference signal, the business\u2019s duty to process that request is governed by \u00a7 7025:\n\n*   **Scope of Application:** The business must treat the signal as a valid request to opt-out of the sale or sharing of personal information for the specific browser or device used by the consumer (\u00a7 7025(b)(1)).\n*   **Consumer Profiles:** The business must apply the opt-out to any consumer profile that the business associates with that specific browser or device, including pseudonymous profiles (\u00a7 7025(b)(1)).\n*   **Limitation to Known Information:** The business is only required to apply the opt-out to the information linked to that specific browser identifier. It is not required (and generally unable) to apply the request to other account information if the business cannot associate the browser with a known consumer profile (\u00a7 7025(b)(7)(A)).\n*   **No Additional Requirements:** The business may not require the consumer to provide additional information beyond what is necessary to send the signal to process the request. While a business may offer the option for a consumer to provide identifying information to help facilitate the request\u2014such as applying it to offline sale or sharing\u2014the business must still honor the signal for the browser or device even if the consumer chooses not to provide that additional information (\u00a7 7025(b)(2)).\n\n**Illustrative Example:**\nAs provided in \u00a7 7025(b)(7)(A), if a user (Caleb) visits a website with the signal enabled but is not logged into an account and the business cannot associate the browser with any other profile, the business must stop selling or sharing personal information linked to that browser identifier. However, the business is not required to apply the opt-out to any other account information because the connection between the browser and that account is unknown.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8867,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, accurate, and provides a comprehensive explanation that aligns perfectly with the ground truth. It correctly identifies the scope of the opt-out preference signal for unauthenticated users, distinguishes between browser-linked profiles and unknown account profiles, and correctly cites the relevant regulatory provisions (noting that while the candidate cites \u00a7 7025(b) instead of (c), the content accurately reflects the requirements of the CCPA regulations regarding opt-out signals). The inclusion of the illustrative example further clarifies the application of the rule.",
    "model_refusal": false,
    "execution_time_sec": 8.605,
    "retrieved_sources": [
      {
        "parent_id": "v2026_sec7025_p2",
        "section": "7025",
        "section_title": "Opt-out Preference Signals.",
        "rerank_score": 0.6406,
        "snippet": "\u00a7 7025. Opt-out Preference Signals.\n(b):\n(1)\nThe business shall treat the opt-out preference signal as a valid request\nto opt-out of sale/sharing submitted pursuant to Civil Code s..."
      },
      {
        "parent_id": "v2023_sec7025_p2",
        "section": "7025",
        "section_title": "Opt-out Preference Signals.",
        "rerank_score": 0.6158,
        "snippet": "\u00a7 7025. Opt-out Preference Signals.\n(b):\n(1)\nThe business shall treat the opt-out preference signal as a valid request\nto opt-out of sale/sharing submitted pursuant to Civil Code s..."
      }
