    "key_term_recall": 0.5,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Yes. Under \u00a7 7220(e)(1), a business may provide a consolidated Pre-use Notice when using a single ADMT for multiple purposes. The regulation specifically highlights an employer using productivity monitoring software to determine work allocation/compensation and demotions as a valid consolidated notice use case, provided all required \u00a7 7220 disclosures are included for each purpose.",
    "generated_answer": "Yes, an employer may provide a single consolidated Pre-use Notice for these purposes. \n\nAccording to **\u00a7 7220(e)(1)**, a business may provide a consolidated Pre-use Notice for the use of a single Automated Decision-Making Technology (ADMT) for multiple purposes. The regulation explicitly cites your scenario as an example:\n\n*   An employer may provide a consolidated Pre-use Notice to an employee that addresses the proposed use of \"productivity monitoring software to determine the employee\u2019s allocation/assignment of work and compensation, and to determine which employees will be demoted\" (**\u00a7 7220(e)(1)**).\n\nTo remain in compliance, any consolidated Pre-use Notice must include all the information required by the Article for each of the business\u2019s proposed uses of ADMT (**\u00a7 7220(e)**). This includes, but is not limited to:\n\n*   **Content Requirements**: The notice must include a plain language explanation of the specific purposes, descriptions of the consumer's right to opt-out or appeal, the right to access the ADMT, an anti-retaliation statement, and detailed information on how the ADMT works and affects the decision-making process (**\u00a7 7220(c)(1)-(5)**).\n*   **Presentation Requirements**: The notice must be presented prominently and conspicuously at or before the point when the personal information is collected for these purposes, or before the processing begins if the data was collected previously (**\u00a7 7220(b)(2)**). It must also be presented in the manner in which the business primarily interacts with the employee (**\u00a7 7220(b)(3)**).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9123,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
