    "eval_id": "eval_7080_004",
    "query": "Can a music streaming business charge free-tier users $5 per month if they choose to opt-out of the sale or sharing of their personal information?",
    "target_section": "7080",
    "expected_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "actual_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Standard regulatory policy lookup targeting active 2026 regulations.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.5,
    "key_term_recall": 0.25,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7080(d)(1), restricting opt-out rights exclusively to paying subscribers ($5/month) while forcing free-tier users to allow data sale/sharing is discriminatory and prohibited, UNLESS the business can prove through a good-faith calculation that the $5 monthly payment is reasonably related to the value of the consumer's data to the business.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, whether a music streaming business can charge a fee or differentiate service based on an opt-out request depends on whether the practice is considered \"discriminatory\" under **\u00a7 7080**.\n\n### Requirements for Price or Service Differences\nUnder **\u00a7 7080(a)**, a price or service difference is prohibited if the business treats a consumer differently because they exercised a right conferred by the CCPA (such as the right to opt-out of the sale or sharing of personal information).\n\nHowever, a business may offer such a difference if it meets the following criteria:\n\n*   **Reasonable Relationship to Data Value**: Per **\u00a7 7080(b)**, a price or service difference is non-discriminatory if it is \"reasonably related to the value of the consumer\u2019s data.\" \n*   **Good-Faith Estimate**: The business must be able to calculate a good-faith estimate of the value of the consumer's data. If the business cannot calculate this value or demonstrate that the price difference is reasonably related to that value, it is prohibited from offering the price or service difference (**\u00a7 7080(b)**).\n*   **Transparency**: Per **\u00a7 7080(e)**, the business must notify consumers of any price or service difference it offers, in accordance with section 7016.\n\n### Application to the Scenario\nThe provided regulatory text specifically addresses your question in **\u00a7 7080(d)(1)**:\n\n> \"Example 1: A music streaming business offers a free service as well as a premium service that costs $5 per month. If only the consumers who pay for the music streaming service are allowed to opt-out of the sale or sharing of their personal information, then the practice is discriminatory, unless the $5-per-month payment is reasonably related to the value of the consumer\u2019s data to the business.\"\n\n**Summary:** \nThe business **cannot** simply charge the fee because the user opted out. To avoid being considered discriminatory under **\u00a7 7080**, the business must be able to demonstrate that the $5-per-month charge is **reasonably related to the value of the consumer\u2019s data** to the business. \n\n*Note: If the price difference is the \"direct result of compliance with a state or federal law,\" it is not considered discriminatory (**\u00a7 7080(g)**).*",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8508,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, complete, and perfectly aligned with the ground truth. It correctly identifies the relevant regulatory section (\u00a7 7080(d)(1)), explains the prohibition against discriminatory practices, and accurately articulates the 'good-faith' exception regarding the value of the consumer's data.",
    "model_refusal": false,
    "execution_time_sec": 7.172,
    "retrieved_sources": [
