    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7050(e), a person who lacks a contract complying with \u00a7 7051 is NOT a service provider or contractor under the CCPA. Consequently, the disclosure of personal data to that vendor may be legally classified as a sale or sharing of personal information, requiring the business to offer opt-out rights.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, if a business transfers personal information to a person or entity that does not have a contract complying with **\u00a7 7051, subsection (a)**, the consequences are as follows:\n\n*   **Loss of Status**: The person or entity will not be considered a \"service provider\" or \"contractor\" under the CCPA (**\u00a7 7050(e)**).\n*   **Characterization as Sale or Sharing**: Because the entity does not qualify as a service provider or contractor, the business\u2019s disclosure of personal information to that person may be legally considered a \"sale\" or \"sharing\" of personal information (**\u00a7 7050(e)**).\n*   **Consumer Opt-Out Requirement**: As a result of the disclosure being categorized as a sale or sharing, the business must provide the consumer with the right to opt-out of such sale or sharing (**\u00a7 7050(e)**).\n\nPlease note that the provided text does not contain the specific text of **\u00a7 7051**, but it explicitly references that the absence of a contract meeting those requirements results in the classifications listed above.",
