    "precision_at_k": 0.4,
    "key_term_recall": 0.6,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7001(fff), 'train' means the process through which a technology discovers underlying patterns, learns a series of actions, or is taught to generate a desired output. This includes adjusting algorithmic parameters for ADMT, improving how a machine-learning model learns, and iterating datasets fed into ADMT.",
    "generated_answer": "Under California privacy regulations, the term \"train\" is defined in **\u00a7 7001(fff)**.\n\n### Legal Definition of \"Train\"\nFor the purposes of the requirements regarding Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT), to **\"train\"** means the process through which a technology:\n*   Discovers underlying patterns;\n*   Learns a series of actions; or\n*   Is taught to generate a desired output.\n\n### Examples of Training\n**\u00a7 7001(fff)** provides the following specific examples of what constitutes training:\n*   Adjusting the parameters of an algorithm used for ADMT;\n*   Improving the algorithm that determines how a machine-learning model learns; and\n*   Iterating the datasets fed into ADMT.\n\n***\n\n**Regulatory Context Note:** This definition is specifically applicable to the requirements set forth in **\u00a7 7150(b)(6)** and **\u00a7 7153**. Additionally, **\u00a7 7153(b)** clarifies that the requirements for businesses processing personal information to train ADMT apply only to ADMT that has been trained using personal information.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8701,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, fully aligned with the ground truth, and provides additional helpful context regarding the regulatory application of the definition without introducing hallucinations.",
