    "generated_answer": "Based on the regulatory context provided, there is no information regarding the EU AI Act. The provided text is strictly limited to requirements under California privacy law (CCPA/CPRA) regarding Automated Decisionmaking Technology (ADMT), specifically:\n\n*   **Requirements for Businesses Providing ADMT to Others**: Under **\u00a7 7153(a)**, a business that makes ADMT available to a \"recipient-business\" to facilitate a significant decision (as set forth in \u00a7 7150, subsection (b)(3)) must provide the recipient-business with all available facts necessary for the recipient-business to perform its own risk assessment. This requirement applies only to ADMT trained using personal information (**\u00a7 7153(b)**).\n*   **Compliance Timeline**: Under **\u00a7 7200(b)**, businesses using ADMT for significant decisions prior to January 1, 2027, must be compliant with the relevant regulatory articles by that date. For ADMT usage on or after January 1, 2027, a business must be in compliance at any time it uses ADMT for a significant decision (**\u00a7 7200(b)**).\n*   **General Applicability**: Under **\u00a7 7200(a)**, a business using ADMT to make a significant decision concerning a consumer must comply with the requirements of the article.\n\nThe provided context does not contain any information concerning European Union law or the specific obligations imposed by the EU AI Act.",
