    "key_term_recall": 0.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7025(c)(5) and Example (C) in \u00a7 7025(c)(7), where a consumer is known to the business and has previously submitted an opt-out preference signal linked to their account, the business shall NOT interpret the absence of an opt-out preference signal on a different device or browser as consent to opt-in or sell personal information.",
    "generated_answer": "Based on the provided regulatory context, **no**, the business may not interpret the absence of an opt-out preference signal as consent to sell or share personal information.\n\nSpecifically, the regulations dictate the following regarding this scenario:\n\n*   **Prohibition on interpreting the absence of a signal**: Pursuant to **\u00a7 7025(b)(5)**, where a consumer is known to the business, the business shall not interpret the absence of an opt-out preference signal after the consumer previously sent one as consent to opt-in to the sale or sharing of personal information.\n*   **Illustrative Example**: **\u00a7 7025(b)(7)(C)** provides an example of this specific situation: When a consumer (Angela) who has enabled an opt-out preference signal logs into her account using a different device that does *not* have the signal enabled, the business \"shall not interpret the absence of the opt-out preference signal as consent to opt-in to the sale of personal information.\"\n*   **Requirement to process requests**: A business is required to treat an opt-out preference signal as a valid request to opt-out of sale/sharing for the browser or device and any consumer profile associated with that browser or device, including pseudonymous profiles. If the business knows the consumer, it shall also treat the signal as a valid request to opt-out for the consumer as well (**\u00a7 7025(b)(1)**).\n\nIn summary, the business must honor the prior request to opt-out and cannot treat the transition to a device lacking the signal as an affirmative consent to resume the sale or sharing of the consumer's personal information.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9127,
    "llm_judge_score": 4.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally accurate and provides a correct conclusion. However, it contains a minor citation error: it cites \u00a7 7025(b)(5) and (b)(7)(C), whereas the correct citations (as noted in the ground truth) are \u00a7 7025(c)(5) and (c)(7)(C). Despite this technical citation error, the substantive legal analysis is excellent and fully aligned with the ground truth.",
