    "key_term_recall": 0.0,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "No. Under \u00a7 7025(d), neither the business nor the platform/technology sending the opt-out preference signal shall use, disclose, or retain any personal information collected in connection with sending or processing the signal for ANY purpose other than sending or processing the opt-out preference signal.",
    "generated_answer": "No, businesses are not permitted to use, disclose, or retain personal information collected from a consumer in connection with the sending or processing of an opt-out preference signal for any purpose other than facilitating that request.\n\nAccording to the provided regulatory context:\n\n*   **Prohibited Uses**: Per **\u00a7 7025(d)**, a business and the platform, technology, or mechanism sending the signal shall not use, disclose, or retain any personal information collected in connection with the sending or processing of the opt-out preference signal for any purpose other than sending or processing the opt-out request itself.\n*   **Minimal Data Collection**: Per **\u00a7 7025(b)(2)**, a business shall not require a consumer to provide additional information beyond what is necessary to send the signal. While a business may offer the option to provide additional information to facilitate the request (e.g., to apply the opt-out to offline sales), any such information provided by the consumer must not be used, disclosed, or retained for any purpose other than processing the opt-out request.\n\nTherefore, using such information for marketing analytics is strictly prohibited under **\u00a7 7025(d)**.",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.9212,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
    "llm_judge_reasoning": "The candidate answer is legally precise, directly addresses the query, and correctly identifies the prohibition under \u00a7 7025(d). It accurately reflects the ground truth and provides additional relevant context regarding the scope of the regulation.",
