  {
    "eval_id": "eval_7080_001",
    "query": "What foundational legal threshold determines whether a price or service difference offered by a business is non-discriminatory under CCPA?",
    "target_section": "7080",
    "expected_route": "POLICY_LOOKUP",
    "actual_route": "HYBRID_BOTH",
    "route_correct": false,
    "route_reasoning": "Query contains ambiguous update keyword 'difference'. Routing to hybrid policy + diff lookup.",
    "answerable": true,
    "abstained": false,
    "section_hit": true,
    "hit_in_top_1": true,
    "precision_at_k": 0.5,
    "key_term_recall": 0.75,
    "citation_valid": true,
    "ground_truth_answer": "Under \u00a7 7080(b), a price or service difference is non-discriminatory only if it is reasonably related to the value of the consumer's data. If a business is unable to calculate a good-faith estimate of the value of the data or show that the price/service difference is reasonably related to that value, it is legally prohibited from offering the price or service difference.",
    "generated_answer": "To determine whether a price or service difference is non-discriminatory under the CCPA/CPRA, businesses must adhere to the foundational requirement of **reasonable value connection** as established in \u00a7 7080.\n\n### Foundational Legal Threshold\nA price or service difference is considered non-discriminatory if it is **reasonably related to the value of the consumer\u2019s data** to the business (\u00a7 7080(b)). \n\n*   **Good-Faith Estimate:** If a business cannot calculate a good-faith estimate of the value of the consumer\u2019s data, or if it cannot demonstrate that the price or service difference is reasonably related to that value, the business is prohibited from offering the price or service difference (\u00a7 7080(b)).\n*   **Prohibition on Retaliation:** A price or service difference is inherently discriminatory and prohibited under Civil Code section 1798.125 if it treats a consumer differently because they exercised a right conferred by the CCPA or its regulations (\u00a7 7080(a)).\n\n### Recent Regulatory Updates\nThe regulatory framework for non-discriminatory practices has been expanded to reflect the broader scope of consumer rights regarding Automated Decision-Making Technology (ADMT):\n\n*   **Expanded Scope of Permitted Denials:** Under \u00a7 7080(c), the list of requests that a business may deny\u2014without those denials being considered discriminatory\u2014has been updated to include:\n    *   Requests to access ADMT.\n    *   Requests to opt-out of ADMT.\n*   **Consistent Application:** These additions were integrated into the 2026 regulations to ensure that businesses can manage their services in relation to these new consumer rights, provided the denials remain within the reasons permitted by the CCPA or related regulations (\u00a7 7080(c)).\n\n### Statutory Exceptions\nNot all differences in price or service are subject to the \"value of data\" threshold. A price or service difference shall not be considered discriminatory if it is the **direct result of compliance with a state or federal law** (\u00a7 7080(g)). Additionally, charging a reasonable fee as permitted under Civil Code section 1798.145, subdivision (h)(3), is not considered a financial incentive subject to these regulations (\u00a7 7080(f)).",
    "semantic_similarity": 0.8566,
    "llm_judge_score": 5.0,
